ganization  selling you the tickets and  running the timetable but but in terms  of not going back to british reality  again one of the the the supposed  impacts of privatization was that  operating companies would have  um you know their freedom for certain  levels of innovation and what have you  isn't there a possibility here that  great british rail will turn into a  british rail sort of leviathan well  absolutely not what we  uh plan to do with this uh you're right  we want to keep the sort of  the private uh you know initiatives the  capital  the innovation all of that in there but  you've got to have somebody on a railway  which is you know 20 000 miles of track  organizing a timetable organizing the  ticketing  making sure that there's a single body  so you say when something goes wrong you  can say  who is in charge here and there's an  organization and it will be great  british railways  who are in charge so this reform which  is like i see has been widely  welcomed by industry experts this  morning  uh i think is is is overdue i think you  know as a commuter myself i just want  a railway that works i want the  ticketing to be  straightforward and and simple uh and we  won't be going back to the days of  british rail and terrible sandwiches and  all the rest of it  it will be a new organization okay so so  what is the direct benefit for  for passengers then i mean certainly you  want trains to be on time you want them  to be clean obviously i mean that goes  without saying  in terms of the ticketing i mean it is a  bit of a nightmare at the moment  and it's expensive yeah yeah is that  going to change well  that's right at the moment you turn up  at a station there are two or three  different ticket machines you're  supposed to know which one  uh to get if you buy the wrong ticket  you can't use it on this service all of  that  gets simplified and you end up with a  railway where  you buy the ticket it can only be the  right ticket because there's only a  uh uh you know you you're buying yes  great british railways  and whichever route you need to take  you'll be able to take  but actually the other thing is crazy  you stand by these machines they print  out goodness knows how many tickets for  a single journey  why we even got paper tickets i mean it  should be contactless and one of the  great things we'll be introducing  um in june next month are flexible  season tickets  because we know that things have changed  people are working from home a lot more  if you're traveling two or three days a  week uh you'll be able to use these  flexible season tickets  that's only possible because we've  started now on the route towards the  screen i wonder if that is is this going  to guarantee  that it's going to be cheaper though at  the moment if i travel into london i  live about a hundred miles away so if i  travel in on the train  use one particular network one company  it's cheaper than if i get a ticket that  covers all the companies yeah  now is that price and gonna go up to  match the multi company thing or will it  stay at the lower level no well the  great thing is um  depending on how many days you're  traveling in you might well be able to  use the flexible season ticket to do  this you don't have to work out how many  days you're going to be traveling  this week in advance in order to take  advantage of that  and also because it will all be under  great british railways  you can take whichever route you wish to  take if there were different  options and usually to get from a to b  there often can be  different routes to get there because  it's all part of the same thing  one organization setting the fares  setting the timetable  it's a simplification which i think  people will broadly welcome in this  country  okay um sorry about something which  isn't simple which is the the current  state of the covid pandemic  can talk about the indian indian variant  um  because we've still got flights direct  flights coming 